This is the Rust ecosystem's convention: the MIT licence is short and
maximally permissive, and Apache-2.0 adds an explicit patent grant that some
organisations require before they will use or contribute to a project. You may
comply with either one; you do not have to comply with both.

Unless you explicitly state otherwise, any contribution intentionally submitted
for inclusion in Girsa by you shall be dual-licensed as above, without any
additional terms or conditions.


WHY THIS LICENCE AND NOT ANOTHER
--------------------------------

Girsa shares crates with Ksav (see the sefer-crates repository), and Ksav is
MIT OR Apache-2.0. The shared-crate design is the whole reason the two-app
system works, and it forces the licence: a copyleft dependency pulled in here
would relicense Ksav through the shared code.

The three most relevant prior-art projects are all copyleft — Zayit
(AGPL-3.0 Section 7b), HebMorph (AGPL) and Sefaria-ElasticSearch (GPL-3.0).
Read them; copy nothing from them. Otzaria is UNLICENSE and safe to draw from.


THE CORPUS IS NOT COVERED BY THIS LICENCE
-----------------------------------------

No corpus text is committed to this repository. Texts are downloaded at first
run and each carries its own source and licence in its metadata (spec.md 13).
This licence covers the software only.
